#* {{RQ:Ouida Under Two Flags|volume=II|chapter=Cigarette en Bienfaitrice|page=121|passage=[S]he was angry with herself; and, for that, sang the more loudly the most wicked and '''''risqué''''' of her slang songs, that gave the morals of a {{w|Messalina}} in the language of a fish-wife, and yet had an inalienable, mischievous, contagious, dauntless French grace in it withal.}}
#* {{RQ:Corelli Sorrows of Satan|chapter=I|page=5|passage=Never blame society,—it buys books! Now if you could write a smart love-story, slightly '''''risqué''''',—even a little more than '''''risqué''''' for that matter; that is the sort of thing that suits the present age.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=[[w:Stopford Brooke (chaplain)|Stopford A[ugustus] Brooke]]|chapter={{w|Much Ado About Nothing}}|title=Ten More Plays of Shakespeare|location=London|publisher=[[w:Constable & Robinson|Constable and Company]]|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cu31924079612085/page/n21/mode/1up|oclc=1900067|passage=Therefore he placed along with this couple who had become one, Margaret, the gamesome lady's-maid, who is '''risquée''' in her talk with her mistress, and has Borachio as a lover—so anxious is [[w:William Shakespeare|{{quote-gloss|William}} Shakespeare]] for variety of character and of life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Vicente Blasco Ibáñez|Vicente Blasco Ibañez]]|chapter=Two Women Scold a Pope|translator=w:Arthur Livingston|title=The Pope of the Sea: An Historical Medley|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:E. P. Dutton|E[dward] P[ayson] Dutton & Company]],{{nb...|681 Fifth Avenue}}|year=1927|section=part 1 (The City of the Three Keys)|page=119|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/popeofseahistori00blas/page/119/mode/1up|oclc=1850578|passage=The housewives of Siena could not believe that such a woman could be very important in the world; but beyond the limits of her native town, in Florence, in Rome, Catherine was already famous for her gifts of prophecy. A woman of strong will, rough and '''risquée''' of speech, she declared herself appointed of God to achieve the great purpose of her time—the return of the Holy See to Rome.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=10 April 1943|author=w:Dorothy Kilgallen|title=The Magic Moniker|journal=[[w:Collier's|Collier’s]]|location=Springfield, Oh.|publisher=[[w:Crowell-Collier Publishing Company|The Crowell-Collier Publishing Company]]|volume=111|issue=15|page=51|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/colliers111aprspri/page/n138/mode/1up|column=1|issn=2161-6469|oclc=8755061|passage=Fashion magazines and advertising photographers usually cast her {{quote-gloss|{{w|Candy Jones}}}} in tweeds, but she prefers long slinky dresses slit to the knee and black lace stockings. "I figure I might as well be gay and '''risquée''' while I can," she says, with a little tremor in her boyish voice. "I'll be old soon."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Page Bryant|chapter=A Time for Amulet Making|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TEEyqDbex08C&pg=PT264|title=The Second Coming of the Star Gods: A Visionary Novel|location=Charlottesville, Va.|publisher=w:Hampton Roads Publishing Company|isbn=978-1-57174-343-5|passage=There was something about Klea that made her different from any other woman Khem and Tiye had met at the Temple. Younger than Taret or Ani, she was more bold and '''risquée'''. They sensed that beneath her lighthearted facade was a strong-willed, self-confident initiate who could not be fooled or intimidated.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=24 January 2016|author=Les Chappell|title=An Uneven Season of ''The Simpsons'' Streaks to an Excellent Conclusion: ‘{{w|Natural Born Kissers}}’ (season nine, episode 25, originally aired 05/17/1998)|work=The A.V. Club|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211012072435/https://www.avclub.com/an-uneven-season-of-the-simpsons-streaks-to-an-excellen-1798186654|passage=But despite being the first episode of ''{{w|The Simpsons}}'' to feature [[w:Marge Simpson|Marge {{quote-gloss|Simpson}}]]'s naked butt, there's nothing particularly '''risque''' about any of the nudity—if anything, the pitchfork-wielding farmer's threat of "ass-forking" feels the most over the line.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023|author=Kate J. Armstrong|chapter=Jewel, Star, and Sea|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LCCeEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT27|title=Nightbirds|location=Crows Nest, Sydney, N.S.W.|publisher=w:Allen & Unwin|isbn=978-1-76052-690-0|passage='Good fashion is never '''risqué''',' she says. 'Only a little daring.'}}

